; The ONE variable: `split_offsets` with more than one entry, on a
; SplitContainer holding more than two children.
;
; Two children need one dragger, and a single offset is all the deprecated
; scalar `split_offset` could ever carry — which is what every other
; SplitContainer fixture exercises. Three children need TWO draggers, and the
; array is the only way to place them: `_update_dragger_positions`
; (`split_container.cpp:651-656`) adds entry `i` to default dragger `i`, then
; clamps it into `_get_valid_range(i)` (`:318-337`) on its own.
;
; The three children all carry `size_flags_horizontal = 3`, so
; `_update_default_dragger_positions` (`:566-599`) hands each an equal share of
; 400 - 12*2 = 376, which truncates to 125 apiece and puts the default draggers
; at 125 and 262 (`:605-617`). The authored -30 and 20 move them to 95 and 282,
; giving three DIFFERENT widths: 95, 175 and 106. An implementation that reads
; only entry 0, or that applies one offset to every dragger, lands on a
; different pair — and equal thirds would prove neither.
;
; `Below` is the same trio at the default offsets, so the two rows differ only
; by the array and a regression cannot hide behind the layout being wrong for
; both.

